(1) lambda_min(M) vs K, and the true covering multiplicity
    criterion: lambda_min(M) < 1  =>  uncovered set nonempty
    {1,...,13} (tight)     lam_min: K=30 +1.0794  K=80 +1.0001  K=160 +1.0000   | true min multiplicity = 1
    {1..11,13,24}          lam_min: K=30 +1.0981  K=80 +1.0008  K=160 +1.0000   | true min multiplicity = 1
    random spread          lam_min: K=30 +1.2628  K=80 +0.3999  K=160 +0.0265   | true min multiplicity = 0
    AP d=8                 lam_min: K=30 +1.3691  K=80 +0.7958  K=160 +0.1499   | true min multiplicity = 0

(2) WHY -- the Toeplitz SYMBOL.  c_d = sum_{v|d} hhat(d/v), so
      f(theta) = sum_d c_d e(d theta) = sum_v sum_n hhat(n) e(n v theta)
               = sum_v h(v theta) = THE COVERING MULTIPLICITY FUNCTION.
    As K -> infinity, lambda_min(Toeplitz) -> min_theta f(theta), i.e. the
    minimum number of arcs covering a point.  So the LP criterion
    'lambda_min < 1' is EXACTLY 'some point is covered 0 times'.
    Check: does lambda_min approach the integer multiplicity?
    {1,...,13} (tight)     lambda_min(K=160) = +1.00000   true multiplicity = 1
    {1..11,13,24}          lambda_min(K=160) = +1.00000   true multiplicity = 1
    random spread          lambda_min(K=160) = +0.02652   true multiplicity = 0
    AP d=8                 lambda_min(K=160) = +0.14990   true multiplicity = 0
(3) THE SHARP CONSEQUENCE: lambda_min(Toeplitz) -> ESSENTIAL inf of the symbol,
    so the LP criterion detects 'uncovered set has POSITIVE MEASURE' --
    strictly stronger than LRC(14), which allows a measure-ZERO lonely set.

    family                 exact uncovered measure   LP can certify?
    {1,...,13}             0                       NO -- measure zero
    {1..11,13,24}          0                       NO -- measure zero
    2*{1,...,13}           0                       NO -- measure zero
    random spread          31624222754504900/259225895372432037  yes
    AP d=8                 251308706854/1974239979075  yes

    => on the three TIGHT families the uncovered set is exactly measure zero,
       so lambda_min = 1 and the LP cannot certify -- yet LRC(14) HOLDS there
       (gap exactly 1/14, attained at the six points p/14).
       The Delsarte LP therefore cannot prove LRC(14) even in principle.
